    {
      "question": "What do the Lab status badges \u2014 Live, Offline, No data, Disabled, Connecting\u2026 \u2014 actually mean?",
      "answer": "The badges describe the background poller's last result for that switch, not the audit you just ran. They are evaluated in strict order, so the first one that applies wins:\n\n\u2022 Connecting\u2026 \u2014 an audit is running against that switch right now (this overrides every other state while it runs).\n\u2022 Disabled \u2014 polling was turned off for this switch with the \"Disable polling\" button in the detail panel.\n\u2022 Offline \u2014 the last poll failed. Select the switch to see the detail panel, which explains the failure in plain English, shows how many attempts have failed in a row, and prints the raw SSH error. The card itself only shows the badge.\n\u2022 No data \u2014 polling is on and nothing has failed, but no successful reading has ever been recorded. This happens when the poller gives up before it ever tries SSH, which it does without recording a failure: most commonly because no SSH credentials are stored for that switch, and also if the switch's vendor has no polling recipe.\n\u2022 Live \u2014 the last poll succeeded and data came back. This is the healthy state.\n\nThe fleet line at the top counts only the last two states: \"N live\" (enabled, no error, has been read successfully) and \"N offline\" (enabled, last poll errored). Disabled and No-data switches appear in neither count, so the two numbers will not always add up to the total switch count.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Live badge",
        "Offline badge",
        "No data badge",
        "Disabled badge",
        "Connecting\u2026",
        "What do the Lab status colours mean"
      ],
      "rootCause": "statusMeta() derives the badge in order: connecting \u2192 !enabled \u2192 last_error \u2192 !last_seen \u2192 Live. last_seen is stamped only on a successful metadata write, and the poller early-returns without recordPollFailure when credentials are missing, so a credential-less device shows 'No data' with zero failures.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "client/src/pages/LabPage.jsx",
          "lines": "76-86",
          "snippet": "// A device is only healthy once a poll has SUCCEEDED. last_seen is stamped on\n// metadata write, so null means \"never got data\" even with zero failures \u2014\n// exactly what a missing-credentials device looks like, because the poller\n// early-returns there without recording a failure.\nfunction statusMeta(d, connecting) {\n  if (connecting)    return { label: 'Connecting\u2026', cls: styles.stWarn };\n  if (!d.enabled)    return { label: 'Disabled',    cls: styles.stIdle };\n  if (d.last_error)  return { label: 'Offline',     cls: styles.stOff };\n  if (!d.last_seen)  return { label: 'No data',     cls: styles.stIdle };\n  return { label: 'Live', cls: styles.stLive };\n}"
        },
        {
          "file": "server/lib/port_poller.js",
          "lines": "167-173",
          "snippet": "if (!creds || !creds.username) {\n    logger?.warn?.(`[port_poller] no SSH creds for vendor=${device.vendor}, skipping ${device.host}`);\n    // No creds isn't a transient SSH failure \u2014 don't enter the backoff\n    // ladder for this; just stamp last_polled_at so the UI shows we tried.\n    portsDb.touchPolled(device.id);\n    return;\n  }"
        },
        {
          "file": "client/src/pages/LabPage.jsx",
          "lines": "237-239",
          "snippet": "const liveN = devices.filter((d) => d.enabled && !d.last_error && d.last_seen).length;\n  const offN  = devices.filter((d) => d.enabled && d.last_error).length;"
        }
      ],
      "userCanSelfFix": true,
