    {
      "question": "What is in a Lab audit result, and why are Speed, Duplex and PoE all showing \"\u2014\"?",
      "answer": "An audit is a live SSH reading taken at that moment (the page labels it \"live SSH pass, not polled\"). It is split into five tabs \u2014 Identity, Ports, VLANs, LLDP and MAC. The four collection tabs carry a count badge; Identity does not, because it is a fixed set of fields rather than a list.\n\n\u2022 Identity \u2014 Name, Model, Serial, MAC, Firmware, Hardware, Host, Vendor.\n\u2022 Ports \u2014 a coloured grid (up / down / unknown) plus a table with Port, Link, Admin, Speed, Duplex, Type, PoE (W), Description and Neighbour, and a PoE budget line if the switch reports one.\n\u2022 VLANs \u2014 VLAN, Name, Status, Ports.\n\u2022 LLDP \u2014 Local port, System, Chassis, Remote port.\n\u2022 MAC \u2014 Port, MAC, VLAN.\n\nA dash means \"the switch did not report a value\", not \"RackTrack failed to read it\". On the lab switches this is expected and correct: they are virtual Cisco IOL devices, which never negotiate a real link and have no PoE hardware, so Speed, Duplex and PoE legitimately read \"\u2014\". A real switch, such as the bench TP-Link, fills those columns in. Empty VLAN and LLDP tabs can also be normal on the lab images \u2014 the page explains why in each case (CoreSW runs a routed L3 image with no switchports; the IOL l2-ipbase image may not support LLDP, since Cisco defaults to CDP).",
      "symptoms": [
        "Speed and duplex are empty in Lab",
        "PoE column shows \u2014",
        "Dashes in the Lab ports table",
        "Audit looks incomplete"
      ],
      "rootCause": "parseIfStatusFor() maps Cisco rows with speed_mbps == null to null; IOL is a virtual image that neither negotiates a link nor has PoE hardware, and the UI's dash() renders null/undefined/'' as an em dash.",
