    {
      "question": "A Lab audit failed with a message about no SSH credentials. What does that mean?",
      "answer": "RackTrack has the switch registered but has no stored login for it, so it never even attempted to connect. The audit fails immediately with HTTP 409, and the message names the exact fix, for example: \"no SSH credentials stored for vendor 'cisco-ios' \u2014 run: node encrypt-creds.js set cisco-ios\".\n\nCredentials are held encrypted on the server, per vendor and optionally per host, with a per-host entry taking precedence over the vendor default (a host entry missing an enable password still inherits the vendor's). Whoever administers the server needs to run that command from the server/ directory; nothing can be entered from the browser, and the Lab view itself is owner-only.\n\nMissing credentials will also leave a device on the \"No data\" badge indefinitely: the background poller skips it, recording only that it tried. Note that \"No data\" is not proof of missing credentials \u2014 it means the switch has never been read successfully, so an unreachable host, a wrong password, or another session already holding the switch's single SSH slot will look the same. Check the server log for the \"no SSH creds for vendor=...\" warning to confirm credentials are the cause before re-entering them.",
