    {
      "question": "Is the \"Success rate\" tile on the Operations tab the scan success rate?",
      "answer": "No \u2014 and this is worth knowing before quoting it. \"Success rate\" is computed across every audited event of any kind (sign-ins, member changes, scans, console commands and so on), as 100% minus the share of all audit-log events that failed. It is a whole-platform health figure, not a scanning figure. It shows \"\u2014\" when nothing has been audited yet.\n\nFor scanning specifically, use the \"Total scans\" tile, which shows the count of successful rack scans, with a \"N failed\" subtitle when there have been failures. One caveat if you are quoting both numbers together: the success count covers scan creation only, while the failure subtitle covers any scan-related failure, so the two are not a clean numerator and denominator \u2014 do not present them as a scan success percentage.\n\nThe Success rate tile turns amber below 90%. Separately, \"Accuracy (feedback)\" is the right/wrong ratio from technicians' \"wrong?\" corrections and turns amber below 80%.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Success rate looks wrong",
        "Success rate doesn't match scan numbers",
        "What does Success rate measure"
      ],
      "rootCause": "successRate is Math.round((1 - totalFails / totalEvents) * 100) over the whole audit_log table, not over scan.* rows; scan outcomes are reported separately as scansOk/scansFail.",
