    {
      "question": "INTERNAL: what account and credential facts must never be given to customers?",
      "answer": "INTERNAL ONLY \u2014 do not surface any of this to end users or customers.\n\n1. Seeded platform-owner credentials are hard-coded in the seed scripts: server/scripts/seed-orgs.js:26 and server/scripts/seed-racktrack.js:27 both define OWNER = { username: 'owner', email: 'racktrackteam@sprintpark.com', password: '[redacted]' }. Seeded org-admin passwords are [redacted \u2014 see the seed script] (username 'sprintpark-admin' or 'racktrack-admin' depending on the script), and seed-racktrack.js prints them to the console on completion. Never repeat these; treat any deployment still using them as an incident and rotate.\n\n2. Deactivation does not revoke live sessions. users.active is checked only in POST /api/auth/login (server/auth.js:832); requireAuth (auth.js:463-499) loads the user row but never inspects `active`. A user deactivated mid-session keeps working with their existing JWT until it expires \u2014 TOKEN_TTL is '30d' (auth.js:39). When support tells an admin \"deactivate the account\", that is NOT an immediate cut-off. To actually lock out a compromised account, escalate for JWT_SECRET rotation and a server restart (which invalidates every token estate-wide).\n\n3. Password reset does not invalidate existing tokens either. /api/auth/reset-password only updates password_hash; there is no jti and no password-changed-at check in requireAuth, so old sessions survive a password change.\n\n4. POST /api/auth/login-with-code (auth.js:925) lets anyone holding a valid 6-digit reset code sign in without changing the password. The code is single-use and expires in 60 seconds, but within that window inbox access is full account access.\n\n5. Verification AND reset codes are written to the server log in plaintext. sendVerificationEmail logs `[auth] verification code for <email>: <code>` (auth.js:385) before any send attempt, and the forgot-password route reuses that same function \u2014 so reset codes appear under the \"verification code\" label. With no SMTP configured the log is the only place the code exists. These info-level lines are mirrored to lib/log-store.js and served by /api/logs to the owner Operations Console; pino redaction does not cover them because the code is inside the message string. Treat the Logs tab as credential-bearing.\n\n6. The org-status gate IS enforced server-side today: requireAuth returns 403 'Your organization is awaiting approval' when orgBlocked(user) (auth.js:479-490). The in-code comment describes a historical hole \u2014 before that gate, isOrgActive() had only three call sites and client-side routing was the only thing holding pending users back \u2014 which is now fixed. /api/auth/* remains exempt by design so PendingApprovalPage can poll /api/auth/me for approval. Do not describe the gate as bypassable.\n\n7. The avatar endpoint accepts index 0-23 (auth.js:1027-1029) but client/src/utils/avatars.js ships only 8 presets (indices 0-7), so 8-23 store successfully and render out of range. Cosmetic, but don't promise 24 avatars.",
      "symptoms": [
        "support asked for the owner login",
        "how do I immediately lock out a compromised account",
        "where can I read a user's verification code"
      ],
      "rootCause": "Hard-coded seed credentials; stateless JWT auth with no revocation list; active flag checked only at login; codes logged via logger.info.",
