    {
      "question": "I re-scanned the same rack but no new scan appeared in my history \u2014 where did it go?",
      "answer": "Nothing went wrong \u2014 RackTrack recognised the scan as one it had already done.\n\nRackTrack identifies a scan by the photo itself, not by when you took it. It fingerprints the image file you upload, so if you send the exact same file again it returns the result it already has instead of analysing it a second time. That is why it comes back almost instantly. You get the same rack ID (RK-\u2026), and your scan list shows one entry, not two. The entry's time is updated to \"now\", so it moves back to the top of Recent Scans \u2014 the original scan time is kept internally.\n\nTwo things to know if you want a genuinely new scan:\n\n- If you have never confirmed this rack's layout, taking a fresh photo is enough. A new shot is a different file, so it is analysed from scratch as a new scan.\n- If you have already confirmed this rack's layout, a fresh photo of the same rack will also come back instantly. Once a rack is confirmed, RackTrack recognises re-shots of it and deliberately serves the confirmed result rather than re-detecting \u2014 the idea being \"show what has already been confirmed.\" This is expected behaviour, not a fault.\n\nAlso note that a cached result is not simply frozen: any corrections you or your team submitted via \"wrong?\" since the first scan are re-applied before the result is shown, so the device names may not be identical to what you saw last time.\n\nIf you need RackTrack to re-analyse a confirmed rack from scratch \u2014 for example because the rack has physically changed \u2014 contact your administrator, as forcing a fresh analysis is a support-side option rather than a button in the app.",
      "symptoms": [
