    {
      "question": "Is there any risk of losing a scan if the server is restarted while I'm scanning?",
      "answer": "A scan that finished analysing is safe: the result is written to disk under the rack's folder before the response goes back, so it survives a server restart. RackTrack also writes down a record of your in-flight scan, which is how the app finds its way back to a scan it lost while the phone was locked or the app was backgrounded \u2014 that claim ticket stays valid for 15 minutes.\n\nWhat can be lost is a scan that was still being analysed at the moment the server went down. That analysis does not resume, and the job is never marked as failed either \u2014 so the app may simply sit on the progress screen until the 15-minute window expires instead of showing an error. If that happens, don't wait it out: just scan again. If the same photo had in fact already been fully analysed, RackTrack recognises the identical image and returns the stored result immediately rather than re-running the analysis.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Analysis failed. Try again.",
        "Progress bar stopped and reset",
        "Scan works on retry with no changes"
      ],
      "rootCause": "scan-jobs.js keeps an in-memory map as the source of truth and persists it to server/data/scan-jobs.json so the resume window survives a restart. Analysis itself runs in Python workers with a per-request wall-clock cap (RACKTRACK_WORKER_TIMEOUT_MS, default 120000 ms) and no restart-resume; only completed scans have outputs/<rackId>/device_unit_map.json on disk.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/server/lib/scan-jobs.js",
          "lines": "16-22",
          "snippet": "// In-memory map is the source of truth; a small JSON file makes it survive a\n// server (nodemon) restart during the short resume window. Entries are pruned\n// after TTL so the file can't grow unbounded.\n...\nconst STORE = path.join(__dirname, '..', 'data', 'scan-jobs.json');"
        },
