    {
      "question": "How do I install RackTrack on my iPhone? I can't find it in the App Store.",
      "answer": "RackTrack is not in the App Store yet \u2014 it is a beta, so it arrives through Apple's testing app, TestFlight. It takes about five minutes.\n\nBefore you start: RackTrack runs on iOS 13 and later. TestFlight itself needs a newer iOS than that, so if the App Store won't let you install TestFlight, that is the reason. You will also need the Apple ID email your invitation was sent to.\n\n1. On your iPhone, open the App Store, search for TestFlight, and install it. It is free and made by Apple. Do this FIRST \u2014 tapping the invite before TestFlight is installed just opens a web page telling you to go install TestFlight.\n2. Find the invitation email from Apple (subject along the lines of \"You're invited to test RackTrack\"). Check spam and the Promotions tab \u2014 it lands there more often than not. It must be opened on the iPhone itself; clicking it on a laptop does nothing useful. If it only lives on your desktop, forward it to yourself and open it on the phone.\n3. Tap \"View in TestFlight\". If that button does nothing, open TestFlight yourself, tap \"Redeem\" in the top corner, and type the redeem code from the email by hand.\n4. Accept, then tap the blue \"Install\" button. RackTrack lands on your home screen with a small orange dot beside the name marking it as a beta.\n5. Open RackTrack from the home screen (you don't need to go back through TestFlight) and sign in. On first run it asks for camera permission \u2014 say yes, or scanning won't work.\n\nImportant if TestFlight says it can't find your invitation: TestFlight matches the invite against the Apple ID signed into the phone. If your iPhone is signed into a personal Apple ID but the invite went to your work address, TestFlight won't find it, and the error it shows won't tell you that is the problem. Contact your RackTrack administrator with the address you want to use and the invite can be re-issued.\n\nFinally, turn on automatic updates once: open TestFlight, tap RackTrack, and switch on \"Automatic Updates\". Beta apps do not update themselves through the App Store, so new RackTrack builds arrive through TestFlight instead. With this on, they install on their own.",
      "symptoms": [
        "RackTrack is not in the App Store",
        "invitation email from Apple",
        "View in TestFlight button",
        "redeem code",
        "orange dot next to the app name"
