      "answer": "This almost always means the new build was signed with a different key than the copy already on your phone, so Android will not treat it as an upgrade. (Installing an older build over a newer one produces the same message.)\n\nFix \u2014 remove the old copy first:\n1. Uninstall the RackTrack that is on the phone: long-press the RackTrack icon and choose Uninstall, or go to Settings \u2192 Apps \u2192 RackTrack \u2192 Uninstall.\n2. Install the new build again:\n   - If you get RackTrack through the App Tester app (you receive an email and a push notification when a new build is ready), open App Tester, pick the latest RackTrack release and tap Download / Install.\n   - If someone sent you the APK file directly, open it from your Downloads and tap Install. You may need to allow \"Install unknown apps\" for the browser or file manager you are installing from.\n3. Sign in again. Nothing else is lost \u2014 your scans, racks and history are stored on the RackTrack server, not on the phone.\n\nIf it still refuses: check you have enough free storage, and re-download the file \u2014 a download that was cut off partway gives the same \"App not installed\" message.\n\nIf it keeps failing after a clean uninstall and a fresh download, contact your RackTrack administrator with the phone model and Android version.",
