    {
      "question": "I was signed in, and now everything I tap throws me back to the Sign in screen.",
      "answer": "RackTrack signs you out the moment the server tells it your session is no longer valid. The usual causes, in order of likelihood:\n\n1. Your 30-day session ran out. Just sign in again.\n2. Your account was deleted by an administrator. Signing in again will show \"Invalid username or password\". Contact your admin.\n3. The RackTrack server's security key was regenerated \u2014 this happens if the server was rebuilt without keeping its existing data folder, or an administrator changed the signing secret. It invalidates every existing session for everyone at once. If your colleagues were all kicked out at the same moment, this is it \u2014 just sign in again. (A routine server restart, or moving the server while keeping its data, does NOT do this.)\n4. You cleared your browser data / used a private window / cleared the app's storage on your phone. Your saved session lives in the browser's local storage; wiping it wipes the session.\n\nSteps to fix: sign in again on the Sign in screen.\n- If it says \"Invalid username or password\", your account may have been deleted \u2014 try \"Forgot password?\", and if that also fails, ask your organization admin whether your account still exists.\n- If it says \"This account has been deactivated. Contact your administrator.\", your account was switched off by an admin. \"Forgot password?\" will not help \u2014 only your admin can reactivate you. (Note that deactivation doesn't kick you out mid-shift; you'll only discover it the next time you sign in.)\n- If you land on a \"waiting for approval\" screen instead of the app, that's your organization's status, not your session \u2014 no need to sign in again.\n\nWhat does NOT sign you out: going offline, poor signal, closing the app, restarting the phone, or your phone's clock being wrong. If the server is simply unreachable, RackTrack deliberately keeps you signed in. Session expiry is judged by the server's clock, not your device's.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Every screen redirects to /login",
        "Signed out mid-shift",
        "Invalid or expired token",
        "User no longer exists",
        "Whole team logged out at the same time"
      ],
      "rootCause": "Any 401 from an authFetch call fires the 'rt:auth-expired' event, which clears token and user in AuthContext, so ProtectedRoute redirects to /login. 401 sources: expired 30-day JWT, deleted user row, and a changed/regenerated JWT signing secret (JWT_SECRET env or server/data/jwt.secret). Session state lives only in localStorage keys rt_authToken / rt_authUser.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/client/src/AuthContext.jsx",
          "lines": "5-16",
          "snippet": "const TOKEN_KEY = 'rt_authToken';\nconst USER_KEY  = 'rt_authUser';\n\nfunction readStored() {\n  try { const token = localStorage.getItem(TOKEN_KEY); ... } catch { return { token: null, user: null }; }"
        },
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/server/auth.js",
          "lines": "32-38",
          "snippet": "function loadOrCreateSecret() {\n  if (fs.existsSync(secretPath)) return fs.readFileSync(secretPath, 'utf8').trim();\n  const secret = crypto.randomBytes(64).toString('hex');\n  fs.writeFileSync(secretPath, secret, { mode: 0o600 });\n  return secret;\n}\nconst JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET || loadOrCreateSecret();"
