    {
      "question": "Two of us share a tablet and signing in as one person signs the other out. Can we both stay signed in?",
      "answer": "No. RackTrack stores exactly one session per browser or per app install. When a second person signs in, their session replaces the first person's \u2014 the first person is silently signed out and will need to sign in again.\n\nThis is also why the app deliberately forgets \"the last rack you were looking at\" on every sign-in: so the next technician doesn't open the previous technician's scan by mistake.\n\nOptions if you share a device:\n- Tap the sign-out icon in the top-right of the Profile screen (it asks \"Sign out?\" to confirm) before handing the device over. This is the tidy way, and it makes sure scans are attributed to the right person.\n- Use separate browser profiles, or one browser window plus one private window, if you're on a desktop or tablet browser.\n- On phones, each person should use their own device \u2014 the installed app has one session slot.\n\nRemember scans are recorded against whoever is signed in, so sharing a session makes the audit trail and scan history wrong.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Signed out when a colleague signs in on the shared tablet",
        "Scans attributed to the wrong technician",
        "Profile shows someone else's name"
      ],
      "rootCause": "AuthContext persists a single {rt_authToken, rt_authUser} pair to localStorage and overwrites it on every successful login; there is no multi-account store. login() also broadcasts rt:rack-id-changed with null to drop the previous session's remembered rack.",
