    {
      "question": "The installed phone app can't sign in at all \u2014 every attempt fails instantly \u2014 but the same login works in a web browser.",
      "answer": "The installed app has the RackTrack server address baked in at build time. If that address has changed since your copy of the app was built, the app is calling a server that no longer exists and can never sign in, no matter how correct your password is.\n\nThis happens in RackTrack's pre-production setup whenever the server's public address changes \u2014 most often when the server is reached through a temporary Cloudflare quick-tunnel, which mints a brand-new address every time the server is restarted, but also after any move to a different tunnel or host.\n\nHow you can recognise it:\n- Sign-in fails immediately with a network-style message (\"Load failed\" / \"Failed to fetch\"), never \"Invalid username or password\".\n- The same account signs in fine in a normal browser pointed at the current address.\n- Everyone using that app build fails; nobody using the browser does.\n\nSteps to fix:\n1. Ask your administrator for the current RackTrack address, and open it in your phone's browser as a temporary workaround.\n2. The real fix is a new app build: your administrator rebuilds and redistributes the app with the current address (Android testers receive it as a Firebase App Distribution update; iOS testers via TestFlight).\n3. Don't uninstall and reinstall the old build \u2014 it will have the same dead address.\n\nAsk your administrator to move to a stable, named address rather than a quick tunnel if the app is being distributed to a team; that stops the problem recurring on every restart.",
      "symptoms": [
        "App-only sign-in failure while browser works",
        "Load failed immediately on tapping Sign in",
        "Whole tester group cannot sign in after a server restart",
        "App worked yesterday, broken today with no change to the account"
      ],
      "rootCause": "VITE_API_BASE is compiled into the mobile bundle (apiUrl prefixes every call with it). Quick Cloudflare tunnels mint a new https://<random>.trycloudflare.com on each start.ps1 run and write it to current-url.txt; the packaged app keeps pointing at the previous one until rebuilt. check-api-base.mjs explicitly warns about this at build time.",
      "evidence": [
