    {
      "question": "[Admin] Nobody can sign in from the web app after we moved to production \u2014 the login page shows network errors, not credential errors.",
      "answer": "Check CORS and the API base before you look at accounts. Three misconfigurations produce this symptom, and all leave the browser reporting a network failure rather than a 401:\n\n1. CORS allow-list. In production (NODE_ENV=production) an origin that isn't listed in CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is answered without CORS headers, so the browser blocks the /api/auth/login response before your app code can read it \u2014 you see a CORS error, not a credential error. Fix: set CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS to the exact scheme+host+port your users load, comma-separated (e.g. https://app.example.com). The variable name is CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS \u2014 server/app.js:260 reads that name. Older docs said CORS_ORIGINS, which appears nowhere in the code and does nothing.\n\n2. NODE_ENV that isn't literally \"production\". The .env loader in server/app.js is a minimal parser: it skips lines that start with #, but it does not strip inline comments \u2014 it takes everything after the first \"=\" verbatim (only trimming surrounding whitespace). So `NODE_ENV=production   # must be production` becomes the string \"production   # must be production\", which fails the === 'production' comparison and leaves the server in dev mode. Put comments on their own line. Consequences of dev mode: raw error details are no longer hidden, and the dev-only mock-route branch activates. Note the precise CORS behaviour \u2014 dev mode reflects *any* origin only when CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is empty. If you set the allow-list correctly but botched NODE_ENV, the list is non-empty so origins are still enforced, and CORS will not be your login symptom; look at cause 3 instead.\n\n3. A stale localhost API base in the web dist. If the bundle was built with VITE_API_BASE=http://localhost:3001 and is then served over an https tunnel, every call is blocked as mixed content. The client self-heals this specific case (client/src/utils/api.js falls back to same-origin when the API base matches http(s)://localhost or 127.0.0.1 but the serving host is neither). That guard is anchored to localhost/127.0.0.1 only \u2014 an API base pointing at a wrong *non-localhost* host is not self-healed and must be rebuilt.\n\nVerify with: GET /api/version (unauthenticated, returns the running commit and server start time) to confirm which build is live, and GET /healthz for liveness. Both are registered before the route modules, so they stay reachable even when the rest of the app is degraded.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Load failed / Failed to fetch on the login page in production",
        "CORS policy errors in the browser console for /api/auth/login",
        "Works from localhost, fails from the public URL",
        "Mixed-content blocked requests to http://localhost:3001"
      ],
      "rootCause": "app.js builds the CORS allow-list from CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS and only reflects arbitrary origins when the list is empty AND NODE_ENV !== 'production'; a non-allow-listed origin gets cb(null,false). Separately, api.js only rewrites an API_BASE that matches http(s)://localhost|127.0.0.1.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/server/app.js",
          "lines": "257-270",
          "snippet": "const _corsOrigins = (process.env.CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS || '').split(',').map(s => s.trim()).filter(Boolean);\nconst _corsIsDev = (process.env.NODE_ENV || 'development') !== 'production';\napp.use(cors({\n  origin: (origin, cb) => {\n    if (!origin) return cb(null, true);\n    if (_corsOrigins.includes(origin)) return cb(null, true);\n    if (_corsOrigins.length === 0 && _corsIsDev) return cb(null, true);\n    return cb(null, false);\n  },\n}));"
        },
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/server/.env.example",
          "lines": "5-20",
          "snippet": "# NOTE: the loader in app.js does NOT strip inline comments ... the value becomes \"production   # MUST be...\" which !== \"production\", and the server silently stays in dev mode leaking raw errors.\n... # The variable is CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS \u2014 app.js:260 reads that name, not CORS_ORIGINS, which this file used to document."
        },
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/client/src/utils/api.js",
          "lines": "15-22",
