    {
      "question": "[Internal] Does changing or resetting a password kick existing sessions out?",
      "answer": "No. RackTrack's sessions are stateless JWTs with no server-side session table, no token version/generation column on users, and no denylist. Consequences when handling a support case:\n\n- After a password reset (self-service or admin-set), any token already issued for that user id keeps working until its 30-day expiry. A stolen or shared device is NOT logged out by changing the password. (The reset endpoint only rewrites password_hash, then mints an additional fresh token.)\n- Deactivating the account (users.active = 0) also does NOT invalidate existing tokens \u2014 requireAuth only checks that the user row exists, not that it is active. The active flag is enforced solely at /api/auth/login. Deleting the user is the only immediate per-user revocation (requireAuth then 401s with 'User no longer exists').\n- Changing an organization's status away from 'active' (pending/rejected/inactive) DOES take effect immediately for existing tokens: requireAuth's orgBlocked() gate 403s every authenticated route except /api/auth/* \u2014 BUT orgBlocked() exempts two cases, so this is not a general containment lever: users with role 'owner' are never gated (owners have no organization), and any user row with a null organization_id is never gated. Suspending an org will not restrict an owner account.\n- The only global revocation lever is rotating JWT_SECRET / deleting server/data/jwt.secret, which signs everyone out at once.\n\nSo for a lost-device or compromised-account report, the correct action is: delete the user (or, for an owner account or a broad compromise, rotate the secret) \u2014 not merely reset the password, deactivate the user, or suspend the organization.",
      "symptoms": [
        "User still has access after password reset",
        "Deactivated user's existing app session keeps working",
        "Support asks how to force-logout a device"
      ],
      "rootCause": "makeToken embeds only sub/username/tenantId/organizationId/role with a 30d expiry; requireAuth re-reads the user row for existence and the org gate but never checks users.active or any token-version field, and no revocation store exists.",
      "evidence": [
        {
