    {
      "question": "My phone's date and time are wrong / I travelled across time zones. Will that stop me signing in?",
      "answer": "Almost certainly not. Every deadline in RackTrack's sign-in is measured by the server's clock, not your device's:\n\n- Your 30-day session is checked on the server when it validates your sign-in.\n- The 1-minute verification and reset codes are timed on the server.\n- The 7-day invite links are timed on the server.\n\nSo a phone whose date is off by hours \u2014 or that has crossed time zones \u2014 will still sign in normally. If you're failing to sign in, look at the actual message on screen instead \u2014 see the entries on wrong credentials, expired codes and network failures.\n\nTwo things to be aware of:\n\n- If your device's date is wrong by months or years, the secure connection to RackTrack itself can fail before sign-in is even attempted. You'll see a connection or network error rather than a password or code error. Setting your date and time back to automatic fixes it.\n- Because codes are timed server-side, a code can look like it \"expired instantly\" if the email took more than a minute to reach you. That's slow mail delivery, not a clock problem \u2014 tap \"Resend\" and use the newest code immediately.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Device clock is wrong and user suspects it blocks login",
        "Verification code appears to expire immediately after arriving",
        "Travel across time zones followed by a login problem"
      ],
      "rootCause": "All expiry comparisons run on the server: code_expires_at is written as server Date.now()+60000 and compared to server Date.now(); JWT exp is produced and verified by the server with jsonwebtoken's default (no clockTolerance option is passed, but the client never validates exp itself).",
