      "question": "What formats can a scan report be exported in, and what does each one contain?",
      "answer": "There are four output formats, all built from the same underlying scan data and all reached from the same address `/api/scan/<rack ID>/report`:\n\n| Format | How to get it | What it contains |\n|---|---|---|\n| **On-screen report (HTML)** | The **View** button in the app (`?format=html`) | Rack ID, scan time, Units and Devices count tiles, and the Port Identifications section \u2014 one card per identified port with the rack and device photos and the console transcript. Self-contained: images are embedded in the file, so a saved copy still works offline. |\n| **PDF** | The **Download PDF** button inside the report (`?format=pdf`) | The same report rendered to A4 by headless Chromium on the server. This is also the file attached when you Share to Slack, Teams or Outlook. |\n| **CSV** | `?format=csv` | Rack ID, timestamp and unit range as header lines, then Port Identifications, Port Command Transcripts (one row per command), and Feedback totals plus every feedback entry. No device inventory table. Sections only appear when there is data for them. |\n| **JSON** | `?format=json` | Everything: rack ID, timestamp, quality note, units detected and unit range, the full **devices** array (label, class, U position, port count, console/SFP/other/connected port counts, PDU power outlet counts), port identifications with their console transcripts, feedback totals and every feedback entry, and the list of report images. |\n\nCalling the address with no `format` returns a small summary (rack ID, timestamp, device count, unit range, feedback total and accuracy) plus links to the HTML, JSON and CSV forms and to the saved report file at `/outputs/<rack ID>/report.html`.",
