      "question": "How do I see which ports are actually in use on the switch right now?",
      "answer": "Open the rack, then open Ports. This screen signs in to the switch over the network and reads it live \u2014 it is not built from your photo.\n\nAt the top you get three tiles: In use (ports with a live link, out of the total), Available (ports you can plug into today) and Identified (ports where the far-end device announced itself over LLDP/CDP).\n\nBelow that are three tabs: Ports, Cables and Ping.\n\nOn the Ports tab you get a picture of the switch front, then filter buttons: All, In Use, Available, Linked, and Errors (Errors only appears if there is something to show). Each one shows a count. The list underneath splits into \"Ethernet (ETH) Modules\" and \"SFP Modules\".\n\nA port can show as Reserved rather than Available for either of two reasons:\n- it has a description saved on the switch but nothing plugged in \u2014 somebody has claimed it; or\n- the switch reports it as shut down, admin-down or err-disabled \u2014 it is not usable until someone re-enables it.\n\nThe Errors filter shows exactly these Reserved ports, so if you want to see them all in one place, tap Errors. Open a port to see its raw status text from the switch (for example \"Not connected\", \"Err-disabled\") \u2014 that tells you which of the two cases you are looking at.",
