      "question": "How do I check whether the switch can reach a particular device or IP address?",
      "answer": "Open the rack, open Ports, then tap the Ping tab (the third tab, after Ports and Cables).\n\nThe card is headed \"Reachability check \u2014 ping, run from the switch\". The test runs from the switch itself, not from your phone or laptop, so it tests the path your equipment actually takes.\n\n1. Type an IP address or hostname in the box (it hints \"IP or hostname \u2014 e.g. 192.168.1.1\").\n2. Tap Ping, or press Enter.\n\nUnder the box is a \"Quick ping:\" row of shortcuts so you don't have to type. There is always one for 8.8.8.8 (labelled \"8.8.8.8 \u00b7 internet\"). A second chip for the local gateway appears only when the switch's management address is a plain IPv4 address \u2014 RackTrack derives the likely gateway by replacing the last part of that address with .1, so it is a best guess rather than a discovered gateway. If the gateway answers and 8.8.8.8 does not, that usually points to a problem upstream of your rack rather than in it.\n\nIf you leave the box empty you get \"Enter an IP address or hostname first.\" If the switch hasn't finished loading you get \"The switch isn't reachable yet \u2014 wait for it to load.\"\n\nOnly ping is offered on this screen \u2014 there is no traceroute button. That is deliberate: on TP-Link switches the tracert command needs a higher privilege than the read-only account RackTrack uses, whereas ping works at that level.",
