    {
      "question": "I scanned my rack and got \"This doesn't look like a server rack\" \u2014 but it is a rack.",
      "answer": "RackTrack runs a quick rack-presence check before the full analysis and refuses the photo if it finds no rack equipment at all. The exact message is: *\"This doesn't look like a server rack. Point the camera at the front of a rack so its devices and ports are visible.\"*\n\nIt is not a rejection of your rack \u2014 it means nothing rack-shaped was detected in that particular frame. Usual causes:\n\n- The photo is of the **back or side** of the rack rather than the front faces.\n- The rack door is **closed**, or the front is completely covered by cable bundles.\n- The shot is too far away for individual device faces to be made out.\n- The rack is largely empty, so there are no device faces to find.\n\n(Photos that are too dark, blurry or tilted usually fail the earlier photo-quality check instead, which gives a different message naming the specific problem.)\n\nRetake from the front, close enough that individual devices and their ports are visible, with the full rack in frame and the camera level with the middle of the rack.\n\nThis check is skipped if you used **Proceed anyway** on an earlier quality warning \u2014 in that case the photo goes through and you may get an empty or badly incomplete result instead of this message. On a MULTI stitch the wording differs: \"No devices were detected on the stitched rack \u2014 make sure each photo shows the front of the rack and the shots overlap.\"",
      "symptoms": [
        "This doesn't look like a server rack",
        "not_a_rack",
        "scan rejected",
        "Point the camera at the front of a rack"
      ],
      "rootCause": "A detect_only pass runs before the full pipeline; if it returns zero devices the request is rejected with kind:'not_a_rack'. The gate is skipped entirely when skipQualityCheck is set by 'Proceed anyway'.",
      "evidence": [
