    {
      "question": "A colleague corrected this scan but I still see the old value \u2014 or my correction isn't visible to my team.",
      "answer": "Corrections behave differently depending on what was corrected.\n\n**Corrections to a specific scan** \u2014 device type, port number, port count, port type, cable colour \u2014 are stored against that scan and are visible to anyone who can open that scan. They are baked into the scan result, the report, and anything exported to your CMDB. If a colleague's correction isn't showing, reload the page; the corrected view is rebuilt after each correction is saved.\n\n**The learned memory that re-applies corrections to future scans** is shared across every Site in your organization \u2014 so a correction made by a technician at one site does improve scans at another site in the same organization. It is never shared with a different organization.\n\nIf you genuinely cannot see a scan a colleague corrected, that is an access question, not a correction question. Scans belong to a Site: as a member or site manager you can only open scans from your own Site, while an organization admin can open any scan in their organization. Ask your administrator to check your Site membership.",
      "symptoms": [
        "colleague's correction not visible",
        "my correction not shared with team",
        "other site doesn't see the fix",
        "correction not showing after reload"
      ],
      "rootCause": "Scan-level corrections live in server/feedback.jsonl keyed by scanId and are overlaid into scan_result.json by writeCanonicalScanResult (triggered via scheduleCanonicalRefresh after each feedback POST) \u2014 visible to anyone who can access the rack. Rack access is tenant/Site-scoped via canAccessRack. The active-learning memory store is partitioned per organization by store.set_org().",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "server/app.js",
          "lines": "7554-7557",
