    {
      "question": "Why does RackTrack keep asking me \"right?\" questions \u2014 and what happens if I just answer Yes to everything?",
      "answer": "The questions are RackTrack asking you to check its work, because it is reading a photograph and can be wrong. Each one is a plain Yes/No. You may see more than one at a time \u2014 on a selected port you can get separate questions for the port number, the port type and the cable colour \u2014 though the port-count question only appears once you have answered the device-type question.\n\nAnswering **Yes** confirms the detection and the question collapses to a tick line for the rest of your visit. Answering **No** opens a dropdown (with an \"Other\" option you can type into) and a Submit button, then you see \"Thanks for the feedback.\"\n\nIf you answer Yes to something that was actually wrong, two things happen: your scan result keeps the wrong value (and so does the report and anything you send to your CMDB), and although RackTrack logs that you confirmed it, it saves no correction and no training example \u2014 so it will make the same mistake next time. Confirming a wrong value is worse than skipping the question.\n\nCorrections stick when you come back to the scan; a plain Yes does not, so a confirmed-only item may ask again next time you open the result.\n\nIf you're not sure, you can simply not answer \u2014 nothing forces you to. RackTrack shows a confidence percentage next to the detected device and cable, and warns you outright when a cable read is uncertain; those are the ones worth checking with your own eyes first.\n\nValues you corrected yourself are called out so you can tell them apart from the model's guesses: a \"Your correction\" badge next to the device type and cable colour in the port detail panel, and a \"\u2014 you set this\" line under the question itself for everything else.",
      "symptoms": [
        "why does it keep asking right?",
        "Yes / No questions on results",
        "Thanks for the feedback.",
        "what happens if I answer yes"
      ],
      "rootCause": "StandardFeedback is the single feedback pattern: prompt + Yes/No, No opens a dropdown with Other, then Submit \u2192 'Thanks for the feedback.'. Answered items collapse to a confirmed line via markAnswered/answeredKeys and never re-prompt. onYes posts is_correct:true, which records a confirmation but applies no override.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "client/src/components/StandardFeedback.jsx",
          "lines": "3-11",
          "snippet": "// One compact feedback pattern used everywhere corrections are collected:\n//   prompt + Yes / No  \u2192  (No) a dropdown of options + \"Other\" (free text)\n//   \u2192  Submit  \u2192  \"Thanks for the feedback.\"\n//\n// If the item was already answered, it collapses to a tiny confirmed line and\n// never re-asks."
