      "question": "After the scan finished I got an \"Image quality warning\" pop-up saying it was taken from a side angle. Is my result wrong?",
      "answer": "Not necessarily wrong, but treat it with suspicion. RackTrack noticed the horizontal lines in your photo (the tops and bottoms of each device) are sloping or fanning out, which is what happens when you shoot a rack from off to one side rather than straight on.\n\nIt let the scan through rather than blocking it, then showed you the **Image quality warning** box over the Overview tab of your results, with the message \"The image appears to be taken from a side angle. Results may not be accurate.\" and these tips:\n\u2022 Stand directly in front of the rack \u2014 not at an angle.\n\u2022 Keep the camera level with the middle of the rack.\n\u2022 Make sure the full rack fits inside the frame.\n\nAt an angle, devices near the far edge of the rack are the ones most likely to be missed or misidentified. You have two buttons:\n\u2022 **Retake** \u2014 takes you back to the scan screen so you can shoot it square-on. Use this if the unit map looks wrong at one end.\n\u2022 **Proceed anyway** \u2014 dismisses the warning and keeps the result you already have. Use this if the unit map looks right; the scan itself is complete either way.\n\n(The same warning box is also used for other image-quality problems, such as a rack whose front is buried in cabling \u2014 the message line at the top tells you which one fired.)",
      "symptoms": [
