      "question": "My upload is rejected \u2014 \"Unsupported file type\" or it fails on a large video.",
      "answer": "Two separate limits apply.\n\n**File type.** RackTrack accepts JPG/JPEG, JFIF, PNG, GIF, WebP, TIFF, AVIF, BMP and HEIC/HEIF for photos, and MP4, MOV, WEBM, M4V and AVI for video.\n\nThere are two checks. In the app, anything that isn't recognised as an image or a video at all \u2014 a PDF, a ZIP, a document \u2014 is refused immediately with **\"Unsupported file type. Upload an image or video.\"** A file that *is* an image or video but isn't in the list above (for example a .mkv recording or an unusual image format) gets past that first check and is then refused by the server, which shows a generic upload error rather than the message above. Either way, converting the file to a standard format \u2014 JPG or PNG for photos, MP4 or MOV for video \u2014 fixes it.\n\nHEIC photos from an iPhone are fine \u2014 the browser can't read them, so RackTrack skips its own checks and lets the server convert them.\n\n**File size.** The hard cap per file is **340 MB**. Long 4K videos are the only realistic way to hit it. If you do, record a shorter clip (the video limit is 120 seconds anyway) or drop the recording quality.",
      "symptoms": [
        "\"Unsupported file type. Upload an image or video.\"",
        "Large video upload fails with a generic error",
        "PDF or ZIP rejected"
      ],
