    {
      "question": "I'm scanning a row of racks quickly and got \"Too many uploads \u2014 retry in 34s\" / rate_limited.",
      "answer": "Scan uploads are capped at **20 per minute per signed-in user** (a token bucket, refilling continuously). The cap is shared across every scan-type upload: single photo, MULTI stitch, video, rack-label OCR, the ticket/incident verification routes \u2014 **and the live camera overlay**. Going over it returns HTTP 429 with **\"Too many uploads \u2014 retry in Ns\"**, where N is the seconds until a token is available. The request is rejected before the file is stored, so nothing is lost and nothing was half-processed.\n\nIf you hit it:\n\n\u2022 Wait the number of seconds shown and scan again.\n\n\u2022 **If you hit it while barely scanning, an open Camera tab is almost certainly the cause.** The viewfinder posts a frame every 0.4 seconds to draw the live device labels \u2014 roughly 150 requests a minute against a 20-a-minute budget. Those background frames fail silently, but they drain the allowance, so the shutter press is what surfaces the error. Close the camera view (the X in the top corner) whenever you aren't actively shooting, or use the **Upload** tab to pick photos from the camera roll instead.\n\nThe limit is keyed to your account, not your network, so colleagues on the same site don't consume each other's allowance. Administrators can raise it on the server via the RATE_LIMIT_UPLOADS_PER_MIN setting; note the limit is tracked per server process, so it isn't shared if the server is ever run as multiple instances.",
