      "question": "The live switch read returns nothing useful, or garbled columns, on our Juniper/Arista/HPE/Aruba switch.",
      "answer": "The live-switch command set is defined for exactly three vendors: **Cisco IOS**, **D-Link** and **TP-Link** (`VENDORS` in server/app.js, mirrored by the `vendors` section of server/console_commands.json). If the vendor sent with the request isn't one of those three, RackTrack silently substitutes a supported dialect \u2014 for the live-switch and console routes that substitute is **Cisco IOS**; the CMDB port-locate path substitutes **TP-Link**. Either way there is no \"unsupported vendor\" error, which is why it reads as a bug rather than a limitation.\n\nSo on a Juniper, Arista, HPE or Aruba switch RackTrack sends commands the switch doesn't speak. The switch errors on them or prints output in a shape the parsers don't expect, giving empty tables, missing speed/VLAN columns, or a probe that ends with \"Probe returned no port rows.\" (One partial exception: the vendor-agnostic \"loose\" neighbour parsers are written to read Aruba/Juniper-style LLDP/CDP output, so occasional neighbour rows can come through \u2014 but only when the Cisco command string happens to be accepted.)\n\n**What to do:** for these vendors, use the rack photo scan for port layout, and use your own tooling or the Netdisco-backed Network view for live state. Adding a vendor dialect is a product change \u2014 raise it with RackTrack rather than retrying.\n\nSeparately: on **Cisco IOL** virtual lab devices, speed, duplex and PoE legitimately show \"\u2014\". IOL never negotiates a real link, so the dash is the truth, not a parsing failure.",
