    {
      "question": "The scan hangs for a long time and then fails with a timeout or \"Internal server error\".",
      "answer": "There is a hard ceiling on how long a single image analysis may take: **two minutes**. If the analysis worker is still busy after that, RackTrack gives up on it, restarts that worker, and the scan fails with a timeout.\n\nOn the phone you'll see the progress overlay sit at 88% (which is normal on its own) and then an error appear on the scan screen.\n\nWhat causes it:\n- A very large or very high-resolution image on a busy or under-powered server.\n- Several people scanning at once \u2014 by default the server runs a single analysis worker, so scans queue behind each other.\n- The worker crashed and is being restarted. If a worker keeps dying within seconds of starting, RackTrack waits longer between restart attempts each time, from 2 seconds up to a maximum of 30 seconds.\n\n**What you can do:** wait a moment and submit the scan again \u2014 tap **Analyze Rack** for a single photo, or **Stitch & Analyze** if you are combining several photos of a tall rack. A retry usually succeeds once the queue clears. If every scan times out, the server needs attention: the worker count is sized to the host's memory (each worker holds all the models), and the timeout itself is adjustable.",
      "symptoms": [
        "Long analysis then failure",
        "\"pipeline worker timed out after 120000ms\"",
        "\"Internal server error\" after a scan",
        "Progress overlay stuck then error"
      ],
      "rootCause": "WorkerPool per-request wall-clock cap defaults to RACKTRACK_WORKER_TIMEOUT_MS || 120000; on timeout the worker is killed and respawned with 2s\u219230s exponential backoff on fast crash loops. Pool size defaults to 1 (RACKTRACK_WORKERS). Uncaught errors fall through to o11y.errorHandler which returns 'Internal server error' in production.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "server/worker-pool.js",
          "lines": "158-162",
          "snippet": "// Per-request wall-clock cap so a hung/OOM inference can't pin a request\n    // (and its worker) forever. Overridable via env; 0 disables.\n    this.requestTimeoutMs = requestTimeoutMs != null\n      ? requestTimeoutMs\n      : Number(process.env.RACKTRACK_WORKER_TIMEOUT_MS || 120000);"
        },
        {
          "file": "server/worker-pool.js",
          "lines": "120-129",
          "snippet": "wlog('error', { worker: this.index, kind: 'worker.timeout', command, timeoutMs },\n            `worker ${this.index} request '${command}' timed out after ${timeoutMs}ms \u2014 recycling worker`);\n          reject(new Error(`pipeline worker timed out after ${timeoutMs}ms`));\n          this.kill();"
        },
        {
          "file": "server/app.js",
          "lines": "493",
