    {
      "question": "The Switch Information screen is empty and mentions a missing ServiceNow connection. Why?",
      "answer": "The Switch Information screen builds its list from your CMDB (ServiceNow), so it needs a saved ServiceNow connection. Without one the server stops before it queries the CMDB and returns: \"No active ServiceNow connection. Add one under Profile \u2192 Connections.\"\n\nIf you are a technician (member or site manager): you cannot fix this yourself. Connections are an administrator-only screen, and it will not appear on your Profile. Despite what the message says, contact your organisation admin and ask them to add a ServiceNow connection for the organisation. Everything else in the app \u2014 scanning, the unit map, live switch ports, cables, topology, firmware \u2014 keeps working without it; only Switch Information and other CMDB-backed views are affected.\n\nIf you are an organisation admin or the platform owner: open Profile \u2192 Connections (or the Connections panel in the Org Console), tap \"Add connection\", choose \"ServiceNow / CMDB\", and fill in Instance ID (just the part before .service-now.com, for example dev266363), Username and Password. It becomes the active connection as soon as you save. Then reopen Switch Information.\n\nNote on precedence: the organisation-wide ServiceNow connection is used first, and only if there is none does the app fall back to the admin's own personal connection. Seeing this message means neither is saved.",
      "symptoms": [
        "No active ServiceNow connection. Add one under Profile \u2192 Connections.",
        "Switch Information shows no switches",
        "Serial numbers show as an em dash",
        "400 response from /api/cmdb/rack/<rackId>/switches"
      ],
      "rootCause": "GET /api/cmdb/rack/:rackId/switches resolves ServiceNow credentials from the org profile then the personal profile, with no env/file fallback; with no active 'servicenow' profile it returns 400 before spawning list_rack_switches.py.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "/Volumes/Racktrack/dark_mobile/server/app.js",
